This is SuperH boot.b for LILO.

You can use this program with LILO as /boot/boot.b.
This program may be distributed under GNU GPL.

					Niibe Yutaka  <gniibe@m17n.org>
					Nezu Bunkyo-ku Tokyo
					JAPAN
					August 2000

* Use of LILO in cross development environment

Here's lilo.conf which I use:
------------------------------
linear
# Cross LILO
# Actually, it's /dev/hda
boot = /dev/hdc
disk = /dev/hdc
  bios=0x80
#
delay = 30
vga = normal
image = /boot/vmlinuz
	label = linux
	root = /dev/hda1
	read-only
------------------------------

As of SH BIOS 0.5, only LBA access is supported.  So, you must need
"linear" line.

It is better *not* to add "compact" line.  As SH BIOS doesn't check
how many multiple sectors can be read, it may not work.

In my cross environment, Compact Flash card is accessed as the device
/dev/hdc at the host.  This disk is shown as /dev/hda at the target
side.  I specify "boot = /dev/hdc" to write the MBR and `map' file in
the disk of /dev/hdc (at the host).  However, since the disk is shown
as /dev/hda at the target machine, special care is needed (or else,
/dev/hdc would be accessed in the target too).  That's the trick two
lines: "disk = /dev/hdc ... bios = 0x80", where 0x80 means /dev/hda
for BIOS.  With these lines, disk geometry uses 0x80 (/dev/hda).

If you mount /dev/hdc at /mnt, the invocation of LILO is:

	# lilo -r /mnt

You need to have /mnt/etc/lilo.conf and /mnt/dev/hdc, /mnt/dev/hda1 and
other files in the target disk under /mnt.

* Building (2026, Debian/Ubuntu)

	# apt install gcc-sh4-linux-gnu libc6-dev-i386
	$ make                  # lilo (static, SH-4), boot.b, boot.b-selk
	$ make lilo32           # static 32-bit x86 installer

Other cross compilers: make CROSS_COMPILE=sh4-unknown-linux-gnu-

precompiled/ holds stripped copies of all four (make precompiled
regenerates them), for use without the cross compiler.

  lilo         installer for the SH target itself, static so it runs on
               any root filesystem (also from a rescue system).
  lilo32       the same installer for a PC.  It has to be 32-bit: lilo
               writes C structs with "unsigned long" fields to sector 0,
               and the first stage reads the second stage sector list at
               offset 52, which only holds with 4-byte longs.  gcc-multilib
               is not needed (it conflicts with the sh4 cross compiler), only
               libc6-dev-i386.
  boot.b       first.S + second.c: generic second stage, boots the first
               image ("boot: first-image").
  boot.b-selk  first.S + second-selk.c: second stage from
               sh-lilo-selk-20051110 as used on the I-O DATA LANDISK
               (and rebadged units such as the Plextor PX-EH40L).  It lists
               the images and waits for a key 1-9 on the serial console
               ('g' enters the gdb stub); toggling the power switch during
               the wait picks image 2.  On a USL-5P (detected by the LED
               register at 0xb0000001 reading back 0x40) the front buttons
               pick image 2-4 without a menu.

The boot code is built with -fno-toplevel-reorder (start() has to be at the
start of the second stage, where first.S jumps), -fno-stack-protector and
-fno-zero-initialized-in-bss; the Makefile checks that start is at
0x8c201000.

* Installing to a disk image (x86 VM)

lilo needs a real IDE/SCSI disk and refuses loop devices.  Instead of
writing to the hardware, attach the image to a small x86 QEMU VM as an IDE
disk, where it is /dev/sda, and run lilo32 there from an initramfs.  Nothing
runs as root on the host.  Needs qemu-system-x86, a static busybox
(busybox-static) and an x86 kernel with ata_piix, sd and ext2/ext4 built
in; Ubuntu's generic kernel works

	$ sudo install -m 644 /boot/vmlinuz-$(uname -r) x86-vmlinuz

The image needs a partition with the root filesystem (sh-lilo itself does
not care which filesystem, the VM kernel has to mount it).  To start from
scratch:

	$ truncate -s 2G disk.img
	$ printf 'label: dos\nstart=2048, type=83, bootable\n' | sfdisk disk.img
	$ mke2fs -t ext2 -E offset=$((2048*512)) disk.img $(( (2*1024*1024*1024 - 2048*512) / 1024 ))k

Then build the initramfs and run the VM (zImage is the SH kernel):

	$ for d in bin boot dev files mnt proc sys tmp; do mkdir -p initramfs/$d; done
	$ cp /usr/bin/busybox initramfs/bin/ # x86 busybox to be used in initramfs
	$ cp lilo32 initramfs/bin/
	$ cp zImage boot.b-selk initramfs/files/
	$ cat > initramfs/files/lilo.conf <<'EOT'
	linear
	boot=/dev/sda
	disk=/dev/sda
		bios=0x80
	map=/mnt/boot/map
	install=/mnt/boot/boot.b-selk
	timeout=50
	image=/mnt/boot/zImage
		label=linux
		append="console=ttySC1,9600 root=/dev/sda1 rw"
	EOT
	$ cat > initramfs/init <<'EOT'
	#!/bin/busybox sh
	/bin/busybox --install -s /bin
	mount -t proc proc /proc
	mount -t sysfs sys /sys
	mount -t devtmpfs dev /dev
	for i in $(seq 50); do [ -b /dev/sda1 ] && break; sleep 0.2; done
	mount /dev/sda1 /mnt && mkdir -p /mnt/boot &&
	  cp /files/zImage /files/boot.b-selk /mnt/boot/ &&
	  lilo32 -v -C /files/lilo.conf
	echo "LILO-EXIT $?"
	umount /mnt
	sync
	poweroff -f
	EOT
	$ chmod 755 initramfs/init
	$ (cd initramfs && find . | cpio -o -H newc -R 0:0 | gzip) > initramfs.cpio.gz
	$ qemu-system-x86_64 -m 512 -no-reboot -display none -serial stdio \
	    -kernel x86-vmlinuz -initrd initramfs.cpio.gz \
	    -append "console=ttyS0 quiet panic=-1" \
	    -drive file=disk.img,format=raw,if=ide

"LILO-EXIT 0" means the MBR and /boot/map were written.  lilo saves the old
boot sector to /boot/boot.0800 of the VM, which is gone afterwards; keep a
copy of the image if you need the old one.  Add -enable-kvm if
/dev/kvm is usable.  The paths in lilo.conf are the ones in the VM; lilo
only stores sector numbers, so they do not matter at boot time.  "linear"
is required (the SH BIOS only does LBA), "bios=0x80" makes the disk the
first one for the boot code.  For a later kernel update on the running
system use the static SH lilo with the same file, with /mnt/ removed from
the paths.

Alternatives:
  - Run lilo on the target (static lilo, e.g. from a rescue system or
    NFS root).  This is the normal way after the first install.
  - Run lilo32 as root on the PC with the disk itself attached (USB-IDE
    adapter), boot=/dev/sdX, disk=/dev/sdX, bios=0x80, the partition
    mounted on /mnt.  This writes to the real disk, so take a backup of it
    first and double-check the device name.
